/*!
 * @defgroup shared Shared infrastructure
 * @brief Code every solver depends on: the mesh, the thermodynamic closure, the
 *        physics formula library, and the runtime support layer.
 *
 * The dependency direction is strictly one way:
 *
 * @verbatim
 *          chromosphere.hpp  +  eos.hpp        (shared)
 *              ^              ^            ^
 *        single_fluid      two_temp     two_fluid
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * No solver directory includes another's solver code, and no two of them share a
 * conserved-state width, a packing convention, or a timestep path. The one
 * exception is deliberate and one-directional: the experimental two-temperature
 * solver reuses `single_fluid/exact_rs.hpp`, the standalone port of the SWMF
 * ideal-gas Riemann solver, because that solver is a self-contained ideal-gas
 * routine and not release physics.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup grid Grid and state layout
 * @ingroup shared
 * @brief chromosphere::Grid — the object that owns all solver state — plus the
 *        two conserved-row index sets and the reusable scratch buffers.
 *
 * There is no global mutable state. Solver functions take `const Grid&`, or
 * `Grid&` when they write a scratch buffer, so several Grids can coexist and
 * every test can construct its own.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup eos Equation of state
 * @ingroup shared
 * @brief Pure-hydrogen Saha equilibrium closure, the CRASH `Gamma1` acoustic
 *        index table, and the caloric inversions and face builders built on them.
 *
 * This is the thermodynamic closure of the release solver. The authoritative
 * state of a cell or face is `(rho, rho u, E)`; the ionization fraction `x`, the
 * electron density `n_e`, the neutral density `n_HI` and the electron partial
 * pressure `p_e` are all DERIVED from it and are diagnostics, never independently
 * advanced variables.
 *
 * See @ref physics_model for the closure equations.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup physics_formulas Physics formula library
 * @ingroup shared
 * @brief Inline closed-form rates and coefficients: collision frequencies, heat
 *        conductivities, radiative losses, heating rates, TRAC, and the hydrogen
 *        ionization / recombination network.
 *
 * Used by the two-fluid solver and by the EOS diagnostics. The release solver
 * pulls only the scalar physical conductivity helpers from here; it has no
 * radiative, beam, coronal-heating or TRAC stage at all.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup runtime Runtime support
 * @ingroup shared
 * @brief OpenMP abstraction and opt-in performance profiling.
 *
 * Neither affects a numerical result. The parallel layer degrades to serial
 * loops when OpenMP is not compiled in, and makes a throwing cell deterministic
 * by rethrowing the exception from the lowest physical cell index. Profiling is
 * off unless `CHROMO_PROFILE=1`.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup release_solver Release solver (single fluid)
 * @brief The production path: a single-fluid field-aligned equilibrium-mixture
 *        model whose conserved state is exactly `U = (rho, rho u, E)`.
 *
 * One fluid, one velocity, one temperature, on a prescribed flux tube. The whole
 * timestep is
 *
 * @verbatim
 *     U^n -> MUSCL-Hancock/Godunov hydro -> U* -> implicit physical conduction -> U^{n+1}
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * and contains no other stage: no equilibrium projection, no carrier-row storage,
 * no TRAC, no beam heating, no volumetric coronal heating, no radiative cooling,
 * and no artificial or numerical conduction.
 *
 * Selected by loading a `Gamma1` table, which the `model_column` scenario does
 * unconditionally. See @ref numerics for the discretization and
 * @ref physics_model for the equations.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup two_fluid_solver Historical two-fluid solver (non-release)
 * @brief The research path: a seven-row ion / neutral / electron carrier state
 *        with finite-rate ionization, three temperatures, and operator-split
 *        source stages.
 *
 * This is NOT the release path. It keeps its own conserved-state width, its own
 * packing convention and its own timestep, and every entry point rejects a Grid
 * that carries a `Gamma1` table. Scenarios `model_gentle`, `model_c7`,
 * `model_flare`, `analytic_canopy` and `pfss_field_line` run on it.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup scenarios Scenarios
 * @brief Initial conditions, boundary conditions, mesh generation and field-line
 *        geometry — one scenario fixes all four.
 *
 * A scenario supplies three callables (chromosphere::Scenario): how many cells
 * to allocate, how to fill the Grid and produce the initial conserved state, and
 * how to refresh the boundary buffers each step. It also decides which solver
 * runs, by loading a `Gamma1` table or not.
 *
 * See @ref scenario_reference for what each one models.
 */

/*!
 * @defgroup driver Driver and run control
 * @brief The `chromo_main` command-line program, its step-cap and snapshot
 *        scheduling helpers, and the files it writes.
 *
 * The driver owns no physics. See @ref configuration for the full command-line
 * and environment reference and @ref io_formats for the output file layouts.
 */
